12 months have passed since the first  kobit-19 cases emerged in germany  meanwhile the virus has claimed more  than 56 000 lives here  more than 2 million have been infected  and the number keeps climbing  over the past year we've had to adjust  our day-to-day lives to a new reality  that we could hardly have imagined  before  it's been a whole year since the  prospect of a family holiday has become  a remote possibility  even impossible a year of keeping our  distance from friends  colleagues even our own families  and every day we witness the  consequences of the pandemic  restaurants shattered inner cities  deserted  public life has come to a standstill  [Music]  welcome to our kobe 19 special i'm  monica jones and berlin good to have you  with us  5608  that's how many new coronavirus cases  were registered in germany over the past  24 hours no big deal we've had worse  now imagine hearing a german saying that  a year ago  unthinkable but 12 months of this  pandemic  have changed us here's how it started  germany first came across coveted 19  here at the offices of this automotive  supplier in bavaria  it's where a 33 year old employee caught  the novel virus  from a co-worker who had traveled to  germany from china  he became patient number one  german officials were quick to assure  the public the country was taking the  virus seriously  but that there was also no cause for  alarm  health minister yen spahn told the  public there's no need for exaggerated  concern  the world health organization echoed  this with calls for simple measures like  more hand washing self-protection is  still the best  possible way we can go about this  but infections spread to every region in  germany  contact tracing became impossible  germany's hold on the virus gave way to  the free spread  of covet 19.  then just six weeks after the first case  was discovered  germany announced the closing of schools  by the end of march a nationwide  lockdown meant the workplace  and the school day had to compete for  attention in homes across the country  the bulk of people in germany seemed to  take the restrictions in stride and  agree with them with the budding spring  germany slowly started opening up again  but by the start of may nearly 7 000  people in germany  had died meanwhile elsewhere in europe  the numbers ranged from more than 24 000  to over 28  000 deaths in comparison with many  european neighbors  germany appeared to have weathered the  height of the crisis fairly well  many people enjoyed the warm summer  months  but then fall arrived and cases began to  rise again  by november a so-called lockdown light  was in place  despite both calls for stricter measures  as well as protests against them  but the mini lockdown didn't work cases  rose at alarming rates  with more and more patients needing  intensive care  by the christmas holidays restrictions  had been tightened again  and after the new year schools and shops  remained shut  the latest extension of germany's  lockdown will continue  through february 14th  after more i'm joined by emelia buma  she's an infectious disease expert at  the bavarian health and food safety  authority  and she's been involved in analyzing the  new coronavirus right from the start  when it first emerged here in germany  good to have you with us  please tell me when did you know what we  were dealing with  it was the very beginning of last year's  january when  a colleague of mine sent me the link to  the who  report on germany cases of unknown  origin  in wuhan china and he asked me matt what  do you think about these cases  are we going to expect something really  big here  a news ask maybe and i said well no i  don't think so definitely not  oh how wrong i was it took a while for  the rest of the population and  authorities to revise the views  i mean i remember that i was flying from  munich airport in late march  so much much later than those first  cases emerged still before the lockdown  though and the security team there was  tested positive and we were all there  without masks  i mean dealing with that situation a  year ago it was learning by doing right  well every outbreak investigation is  kind of learning by doing  why is that so because um  because every outbreak is different you  have to adapt  your methods um you have to adapt  the measures uh you implement to stop  the outbreak  and um yes it's it's always  a unique situation but of course last  year that was  very special very unique and to be  honest also  very exhausting and challenging and and  still  it still is it's it's ongoing uh  and even a year into the pandemic now  we're now in the second year already  we still find it hard to say where a  person  got infected and that's significant why  is it so difficult  well uh with as you said with  ongoing virus transmission and  we have observed really intense virus  transmission and circulation  for several months now it's really hard  to trace back  infection chains why is that say  because on the one hand  it takes some time and effort to  um to reconstruct transmission chains  and well the the public health  authorities have been working at their  limits for some time now  and their priority is to  um to prevent forward  transmission meaning that to prevent  further  spread of covet 19  and also many cases do not know um  where they got the virus from and  there are also some cases who do not  want to say  where they've gotten from yeah and all  of that makes it more difficult and on  top of that of course now we have the  variants  uh that are said to be even more  infectious than the original  virus so it still feels a little bit uh  like learning by doing  how has the pandemic changed your life  well uh in in many ways of course it was  a very challenging year for me  professionally and i have two kids it  was also  with regards to to my private life very  challenging with homeschooling etc  but also uh what i want to say  i have never been so thankful for all  these  brilliant work scientists do  all over the world to develop a vaccine  that's effective against the novel virus  within  12 months or even less than 12 months  it's just brilliant and  what i want to say is that if you get  the chance to  um get your uh coverage shots  when it's your turn to get the  vaccination do it  for your own protection and also  um protection of your fellow  human beings right that's certainly a  new chapter in the pandemic  uh the vaccination male boomer from the  bavarian health and food safety  authority thank you so much for your  time  thank you monica and it's  uh time now for your questions over to  our science correspondent derek williams  when will we get back the normal life as  we know and lived it until the end of  2019 i decided to answer this on air  because  i'm asked the question so often and by  pretty much  everyone and you know what i can't give  you an answer  no one can i can only guess and you know  what else  no one can see the future so what all  the experts out there predict  is just guessing too though some guesses  are of course  more informed than others so so that  said  here's my best guess it's based on  two factors that should be clear to all  of us by now the first  is that the only way to return to some  kind of normal  is through vaccination the second  is that means worldwide vaccination  until we're all protected we all remain  in danger the current flock of  variance has revealed how quickly this  pathogen can cross  borders and the longer we leave it in  wide circulation  even if it's in other countries the  higher the chance  it'll mutate into a variant that can do  an  end run around vaccines so vaccination  programs have to be fast  and they have to be global i see it as a  numbers game how much vaccine can we  make  how quickly to reach herd immunity  everywhere which many experts say is  around  70 of humanity that's around  five and a half billion fully vaccinated  people  um since many vaccines require  two doses let's call it 10 billion doses  for this back of the envelope prediction  i added up  what makers of different vaccines  currently in use  are now claiming they can produce in  2021 which as far as i can tell  is around 8 billion doses so not enough  to round the corner globally by the end  of this year  um granted those numbers change  constantly but there's still  no question it'll also take  time to distribute those billions of  doses so  right now i just can't see a return to  something approaching global  pre-pandemic norms  for for at least a year and and probably  a lot longer  though in individual nations with high  vaccination rates things will likely  grow  more relaxed before that maybe as early  as this summer  best guess  you

